---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
import CodeCard from '../components/CodeCard.astro';

const USDC = '0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48';

// Trimmed from a real run of 5.16.0 on 2026-11-03: the two upgrade warnings init prints for USDC are cut.
const initOut = `$ nuthatch init ${USDC} --chain mainnet
→ resolving ABI for 0xa0b86991c6218b36c1d19d4a2e9eb0ce3606eb48 on mainnet…
  · proxy → implementation 0x43506849d7c04f9138d1a2050bbf3a0c054402dd
  ✓ ABI resolved via Sourcify
  · alias fiat_token_v2_2
  ✓ deployed at block 6082465
✓ scaffolded nest 'usdc' (1 contract(s), 17 table(s)) in .
    nuthatch.toml              config
    abis/                      resolved ABIs
    schema.json                decoded tables + columns
    semantic.toml              what the data means (edit freely)
    views/                     authored SQL derivations (a commented starter to uncomment)
    llms.txt                   how an AI agent queries this index
    .claude/skills/nuthatch/   Claude Code skill (offline, no phone-home)`;

const mcpJson = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "nuthatch": { "command": "nuthatch", "args": ["mcp", "--url", "http://127.0.0.1:8288"] }
  }
}`;

// The tools/list answer of that USDC nest, in the order the server lists them.
const tools: [string, string][] = [
  ['status', 'contract, chain, last and sealed block'],
  ['schema', 'the data model and what each table means; read it first'],
  ['tables', 'the exact tables and columns'],
  ['table', 'recent rows of one table'],
  ['sql', 'read-only SQL over the tip and sealed history, with provenance'],
  ['explain', 'checks a query binds before it runs'],
  ['entity', 'one entity by id'],
  ['diff', 'what happened in a block range, in fixed sentences'],
  ['balance', 'one holder, on a token nest'],
  ['top_balances', 'the largest holders, on a token nest'],
];
---
<Layout
  path="/agents"
  title="Nuthatch for coding agents - Nuthatch"
  description="Use Nuthatch from Claude Code or Cursor: install, init a contract, run the nest, and connect the built-in MCP server. The scaffolded skill and llms.txt come with every nest. Every command checked against 5.16.0."
>
  <section class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="section-label">Agents</p>
      <h1>A contract's history, in your agent, in about a minute.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Nuthatch ships the agent surface in the binary: an MCP server, and a skill and an <code>llms.txt</code>
        written into every nest <code>init</code> scaffolds. Four commands take Claude Code or Cursor from nothing
        to asking questions of a contract's events on your own machine. There is no account, no API key and no
        hosted service in the loop. Every command below was run against Nuthatch 5.16.0 on 3 November 2026.
      </p>

      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <span class="cap">1 · install</span>
          <p>The installer fetches the release binary for your platform, checks its SHA-256 and puts it in <code>~/.local/bin</code>. A fresh macOS shell lacks that directory on its <code>PATH</code>, hence the second line. <a href="/install">Other routes</a>.</p>
          <CopyBlock command="curl -fsSL https://nuthatch-indexer.com/install.sh | sh" />
          <CopyBlock command={'export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"'} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="cap">2 · init</span>
          <p>Point it at a contract. It resolves the ABI, makes one table per event, and writes the files an agent reads. This one took 5 seconds.</p>
          <CopyBlock command={`nuthatch init ${USDC} --chain mainnet`} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="cap">3 · run</span>
          <p><code>--backfill 300</code> starts 300 blocks behind the tip, so there are rows within seconds. The API listens on <code>127.0.0.1:8288</code>.</p>
          <CopyBlock command="nuthatch dev --backfill 300" />
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="cap">4 · connect</span>
          <p>In a second terminal, register the MCP server with Claude Code. It speaks stdio and bridges to the running nest.</p>
          <CopyBlock command="claude mcp add nuthatch -- nuthatch mcp --url http://127.0.0.1:8288" />
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="cards">
        <CodeCard code={initOut} lang="bash" label="init, 5.16.0, trimmed" />
      </div>

      <div class="notes">
        <section>
          <h2>Cursor and the others</h2>
          <p>
            Every MCP client takes the same entry; only the file differs. For Cursor it goes in
            <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> in the project, or <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> for every project.
            Claude Code also reads it from <code>.mcp.json</code> at the project root. If <code>nuthatch</code> is not
            on the client's <code>PATH</code>, use the absolute path, which
            <code>nuthatch mcp --print-config</code> prints for you along with the <code>claude mcp add</code> line.
          </p>
          <CodeCard code={mcpJson} lang="json" label=".cursor/mcp.json or .mcp.json" />
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>What the agent gets</h2>
          <p>
            The tool menu follows what the nest holds. The USDC nest above answered <code>tools/list</code> with ten
            tools; a nest that is not a token has no <code>balance</code> or <code>top_balances</code>, so the agent is
            never offered a tool that can only return nothing. A failed query comes back with a fix hint drawn from the
            real schema, and a <code>sql</code> answer ends with the block it is current to.
          </p>
          <dl class="figures">
            {tools.map(([name, what]) => (
              <div><dt>{name}</dt><dd>{what}</dd></div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>The skill and llms.txt</h2>
          <p>
            <code>init</code> writes <code>.claude/skills/nuthatch/SKILL.md</code> and <code>llms.txt</code> into the
            nest. Both are generated from the decode registry, so they name the tables and columns this nest
            actually has, and both tell the agent to use the MCP tools when they are there and the HTTP API when they
            are not. <code>nuthatch schema</code> regenerates them after you edit the config by hand.
            Open the agent in the nest's directory and it finds them without being told.
          </p>
          <p>
            Building nests, rather than querying one, is a different job with its own skill:
            <a href="/docs/ai/builder-skill/">the builder skill</a> teaches an agent to drive the CLI, and its flag
            reference is generated from the binary and checked in CI.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>What it does not do</h2>
          <p>
            The MCP server runs no model and holds no data of its own: it is a bridge to a running
            <code>nuthatch dev</code>, so with no nest running every tool fails. The agent writes queries; nothing it
            says is stored, because no model output sits in the data path. With <code>--backfill 300</code> the nest
            holds 300 blocks, so a balance it reports is the net flow over those blocks, not a holder's balance. For
            whole history, drop the flag and give it your own RPC with <code>--rpc</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Claude Code 2.1.317 registered the server with the line in step 4. The Cursor entry follows the format
            the <a href="/docs/ai/mcp/">MCP docs</a> give; we have not run it in Cursor for this page.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) clamp(4rem, 9vw, 6.5rem); }
  h1 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; max-width: 18ch; }
  .lede {
    color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 2.2rem; max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .steps {
    list-style: none; margin: 0 0 2rem; padding: 1.4rem 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--bg-elev);
    display: grid; gap: 1.6rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(22rem, 100%), 1fr));
  }
  .steps li { min-width: 0; }
  .steps p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.8125rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 0.7rem; }
  .steps :global(.copyblock) { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .cap {
    display: block; font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.625rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--rust); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .cards { display: grid; gap: 1rem; min-width: 0; }
  .notes {
    margin-top: 3rem; display: grid; gap: 2rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(22rem, 100%), 1fr));
  }
  .notes section { padding: 0; min-width: 0; }
  .notes h2 { font-size: clamp(1.022rem, 2.2vw, 1.2rem); margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .notes p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .figures { margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; }
  .figures div { display: flex; gap: 0.8rem; font-size: 0.75rem; }
  .figures dt {
    width: 7.5rem; flex-shrink: 0; font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.6875rem; color: var(--accent); padding-top: 0.1rem;
  }
  .figures dd { margin: 0; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.75rem; }
</style>
